---
import TutorialLayout from '../../../layouts/TutorialLayout.astro';
import CompileWidget from '../../../components/tutorials/CompileWidget';
---

<TutorialLayout
  title="Custom Jinja Templates"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Tutorials', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Code Generation Workflows', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Custom Templates' },
  ]}
  prev={{ label: 'SymPy & Bode Plots', href: '/tutorials/workflows/sympy-bode' }}
  next={{ label: 'FMU Export', href: '/tutorials/workflows/fmu-export' }}
>
  <div class="mb-6 p-4 rounded-lg border border-accent/20 bg-accent/5">
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-accent mb-2">What you'll learn</h3>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-sm text-text-muted space-y-1">
      <li>The shape of Rumoca's DAE intermediate representation (IR)</li>
      <li>How built-in Jinja helpers walk the expression AST for you</li>
      <li>How to write a minimal template that emits a new target format</li>
      <li>How to invoke a custom template from the CLI</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Rumoca's built-in backends (CasADi, SymPy, FMI, JAX, ONNX, &hellip;) are all implemented as
    <strong class="text-text">Jinja2 templates</strong> that consume a single JSON intermediate
    representation of the flattened model. You can add any target language or output format &mdash;
    MATLAB, Fortran, C, LaTeX, a custom simulator &mdash; by writing a template that consumes the
    same IR. No recompilation of the Rumoca binary is required.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-10 mb-3">Step 1 &mdash; Inspect the DAE IR</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Before writing a template, look at what you have to work with. Compile the model below with
    the <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">JSON DAE IR</code> backend. The output is the
    exact object (named <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">dae</code>) that every template
    receives as its root context.
  </p>

  <CompileWidget
    client:only="react"
    initialSource={`model MassSpringDamper
  Real x(start = 1.0) "Position";
  Real v(start = 0.0) "Velocity";
  input Real u "External force";
  parameter Real m = 1.0 "Mass";
  parameter Real k = 2.0 "Spring stiffness";
  parameter Real c = 0.1 "Damping";
equation
  der(x) = v;
  m * der(v) = -k * x - c * v + u;
end MassSpringDamper;`}
    modelName="MassSpringDamper"
    defaultBackend="__json__"
  />

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-10 mb-3">Key fields in the DAE</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    The IR groups variables by role and lists the flattened equations. These are the fields most
    templates touch:
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm border border-border rounded-lg">
      <thead class="bg-surface text-text">
        <tr>
          <th class="text-left px-3 py-2 border-b border-border font-semibold">Field</th>
          <th class="text-left px-3 py-2 border-b border-border font-semibold">Contents</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-text-muted">
        <tr><td class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-border font-mono text-accent">dae.model_name</td><td class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-border">Flattened model identifier</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-border font-mono text-accent">dae.x</td><td class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-border">Continuous states (map: name &rarr; component)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-border font-mono text-accent">dae.y</td><td class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-border">Algebraic variables</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-border font-mono text-accent">dae.u</td><td class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-border">Inputs</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-border font-mono text-accent">dae.p</td><td class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-border">Parameters</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-border font-mono text-accent">dae.cp</td><td class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-border">Compile-time constants</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-border font-mono text-accent">dae.fx</td><td class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-border">Continuous equations (list of AST nodes)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-accent">dae.when_clauses</td><td class="px-3 py-2">Discrete event handlers</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Each <em>component</em> in <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">dae.x</code>,
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">dae.p</code>, &hellip; carries a
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">start</code> value (an expression AST),
    plus metadata like <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">unit</code> and the
    Modelica-level description string.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-10 mb-3">Step 2 &mdash; Use the built-in helpers</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Expression nodes in <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">fx</code> are deep AST trees,
    not strings. Walking them by hand is tedious, so Rumoca exposes two Jinja helpers that do it
    for you:
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-text-muted leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-6">
    <li>
      <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">render_expression(expr)</code> &mdash; emits a
      target-language string for any expression node (binary ops, function calls, literals,
      variable refs, <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">der(&hellip;)</code>,
      <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">if</code>-expressions, &hellip;).
    </li>
    <li>
      <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">render_equation(eq)</code> &mdash; emits a full
      equation, choosing the residual or explicit form as appropriate.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Both helpers are pluggable: you configure operator tokens, the prefix for derivatives, the
    comment syntax, and so on by passing a <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">cfg</code>
    map. The built-in <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">casadi.jinja</code> and
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">sympy.jinja</code> templates are good references.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-10 mb-3">Step 3 &mdash; Write a minimal template</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Here is a complete template that emits a LaTeX document with aligned equations. It fits in
    twenty lines because the helpers do the heavy lifting.
  </p>
  <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-code-bg border border-border mb-6">
    <div class="text-xs font-mono text-code-text/60 mb-2">latex.jinja</div>
    <pre class="text-sm font-mono text-code-text overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{%- set cfg = {
    "pow_op": "^",
    "mul_op": " \\\\cdot ",
    "der_prefix": "\\\\dot "
} -%}
\\documentclass{article}
\\usepackage{amsmath}
\\begin{document}
\\section*{ {{ dae.model_name }} }

\\textbf{States: } $
{%- for name, _ in dae.x | items %}{{ name }}{{ ",\\\\ " if not loop.last }}{% endfor -%}$

\\textbf{Parameters: } $
{%- for name, _ in dae.p | items %}{{ name }}{{ ",\\\\ " if not loop.last }}{% endfor -%}$

\\begin{align}
{%- for eq in dae.fx %}
    {{ render_equation(eq, cfg) }}{{ " \\\\\\\\" if not loop.last }}
{%- endfor %}
\\end{align}
\\end{document}`}</code></pre>
  </div>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Three things are worth calling out. The <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">cfg</code>
    map at the top re-maps the default operators to LaTeX tokens &mdash;
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">*</code> becomes
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">{'\\cdot'}</code>, <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">der(x)</code>
    becomes <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">{'\\dot x'}</code>. The two
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">{'{% for … | items %}'}</code> loops iterate over
    state and parameter maps by name. The final loop delegates to
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">render_equation</code> so that every operator,
    function call, and variable reference in <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">fx</code>
    is emitted with the configured tokens.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-10 mb-3">Step 4 &mdash; Invoke the template</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Pass the template to the <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">rumoca compile</code>
    command with <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">--template-file</code>. The flattened
    DAE is serialized, the template is rendered, and the result is written to stdout.
  </p>
  <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-code-bg border border-border mb-6">
    <pre class="text-sm font-mono text-code-text overflow-x-auto"><code>{`# Render the template
rumoca compile model.mo \\
  --template-file latex.jinja \\
  --model MassSpringDamper \\
  > model.tex

# Build the PDF
pdflatex model.tex`}</code></pre>
  </div>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    For a drop-in target language (MATLAB, Fortran, C, &hellip;) the same pattern applies &mdash;
    change the <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">cfg</code> tokens, swap the wrapper
    boilerplate, and loop over whichever IR fields your target needs.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-10 mb-3">Where to go from here</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-text-muted leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-6">
    <li>
      Study the full built-in templates in the Rumoca repo under
      <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">examples/templates/</code>. The
      <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">casadi.jinja</code> (~160 lines) is the most
      approachable; the JavaScript backend is the most complete.
    </li>
    <li>
      When your template stabilises, move it into
      <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">crates/rumoca-phase-codegen/src/templates/</code>
      and register it so <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">--backend my-target</code>
      works without the <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">--template-file</code> flag.
    </li>
    <li>
      Combine with <a href="/tutorials/workflows/fmu-export" class="text-teal hover:text-accent underline">FMU export</a>
      when your target needs both a source artifact and an FMI wrapper.
    </li>
  </ul>
</TutorialLayout>
